00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
a northeast valley man facing a number  of charges this afternoon accused of  running an illegal breeding operation  police telling us that 27-year-old  malcolm laster was keeping 17 dogs  inside of an apartment near nellis air  force base in unsanitary conditions joe  moeller spoke to an animal rights  advocate who was beyond frustrated he  joins us live at metro headquarters with  how those animals were discovered joe  police discovered these animals out  there they were called out to this  apartment regarding a child neglect  incident that may have happened there  well when they got there they noticed  these around 17 dogs there some of them  were showing signs of starvation  this was at the end of november animal  control officers found 17 dogs including  five puppies according to police there  were severe sanitation problems in the  unit there was also a french bulldog  found in a bathroom according to the  police report it showed signs of  malnutrition and when it was taken for  medical care ended up passing away  animal rights advocate anula wildrich  reacted to the arrest hopefully people  are more aware if they see or hear  something that they would report it  immediately before it got to this point  if they were aware of it  i'm disgusted as anybody would be the  report states the bulldog had given  birth to five puppies at the end of  october and had not received veterinary  care despite last year's statements that  it had begun to lose weight he told  police she would heal naturally when she  was separated from the pups  back out here live police say laster who  lied about having a breeder's license  will now face a number of charges  including the torturing of an animal  reporting live outside of metro  headquarters this evening joe muller  live local now
 

